=== the history is read once for as long as the lock is held, however many windows ask about
=== it, and read afresh once the lock is gone. Every probe above ran without one, which is why
=== each of them saw its own history.
three windows under one lock:  1 read
one more with the lock gone:   1 read

=== and a read that failed is remembered as a failure, not retried until the lock goes. Both
=== answers below are history-unreadable and not no-transitions, which is what says the cached
=== line was read as a failure rather than as an empty history.
two windows under one lock:    1 read
first:  {"assessed":"unknown","reason":"history-unreadable"}
second: {"assessed":"unknown","reason":"history-unreadable"}

